WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.810
- [Narrator] The steering
wheel, the tire, the seat,

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:06.980
the rear-view mirror: utterly
standard parts of your car.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:09.720
Except, in the mirror's case, it wasn't.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.770
Cars were in use for over 30 years

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:13.850
before the rear-view mirror became common.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:16.350
I guess people just swerved
and hoped for the best.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.390
In fact, the first use
of the rear-view mirror

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:19.960
wasn't even for safety.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:23.134
It was just a way for a
driver to go extra fast.

00:00:23.134 --> 00:00:25.717
(lively music)

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.330
Back in the day, race
cars had a team of two:

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:33.763
the driver and a mechanic.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.670
The driver drove, obviously,
and the mechanic fixed stuff.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:39.920
But here's the critical thing.

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.520
The mechanic had one other duty.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.140
They directed the driver
when the car was clear

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.003
to pass or change lanes.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:52.060
Ray Harroun was a driver in
the 1911 Indianapolis 500,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.510
and he was looking for an edge.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:56.880
Ray figured that if he
ditched the mechanic

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.350
the car would be a lot lighter
and, therefore, faster.

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:03.360
So he borrowed an idea he
saw on a horse and buggy:

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.333
a mirror to change lanes.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.300
He won the race.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.320
But the mirror turned out to be so shaky

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.620
it didn't really catch on.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.370
It took 10 years for someone else

00:01:14.370 --> 00:01:15.940
to try experimenting with the mirror:

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.880
a guy named Elmer Berger.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.930
Elmer was an engineer who
also like to go really fast,

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:22.540
but he wasn't a race car driver.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:23.900
He just liked to speed.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.353
So he created the mirror, and
he called it the cop spotter.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:30.960
So, you see, it's not just for dads

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.500
checking on their kids
fighting in the back seat.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.480
It's really about speeding.

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:36.990
So the next time that you're navigating

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.180
through a sea of cars
on some domestic errand

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.120
going really fast, and you see flashing

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.620
red and blue lights appear in that mirror,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.403
you're using it as the founders intended.

